2. The foldByKey() solution: one empty tally, one function
========================================================================
Alameda      Lee 1,210   Patel 1,200   Rivera 510   write-in 7
San Mateo    Patel 710   Lee 540   Rivera 155
Santa Clara  Patel 1,385   Lee 1,235   Rivera 348   write-in 4

Statewide    Patel 3,295   Lee 2,985   Rivera 1,013   write-in 11

========================================================================
3. Why add_tallies may safely change its first argument
========================================================================
the first precinct's report: {'Lee': 410, 'Patel': 385, 'Rivera': 120}
like foldByKey()   : {'Lee': 1235, 'Patel': 1385, 'Rivera': 348, 'write-in': 4}
   first report now: {'Lee': 410, 'Patel': 385, 'Rivera': 120}  <- unchanged
like reduceByKey() : {'Lee': 1235, 'Patel': 1385, 'Rivera': 348, 'write-in': 4}
   first report now: {'Lee': 1235, 'Patel': 1385, 'Rivera': 348, 'write-in': 4}  <- overwritten!
Same answer both times, but the second call quietly destroyed the
first precinct's report. With reduceByKey(), build a NEW tally.

========================================================================
4. Why not reduceByKey(), aggregateByKey(), or groupByKey()?
========================================================================
reduceByKey(new_total)                       same answer? True
aggregateByKey({}, add_tallies, add_tallies) same answer? True
groupByKey().mapValues(...)                  same answer? True

========================================================================
5. A common mistake: a zeroValue that is not empty
========================================================================
Alameda      Lee 1,310   Patel 1,200   Rivera 510   write-in 7
San Mateo    Patel 710   Lee 640   Rivera 155
Santa Clara  Lee 1,435   Patel 1,385   Rivera 348   write-in 4

Alameda and San Mateo (reports in one partition each) gave Lee 100
extra votes. Santa Clara (reports in two partitions) gave 200, and
that flipped the result: Lee now 'wins' Santa Clara, which Patel
really won by 150 votes. The zeroValue must be truly empty: {}.
